# End-to-End Manufacturing Operations Sample

A fictional precision machining company: two plants, Shenzhen and Suzhou, machine pump housings, valve bodies, impellers and flanges for ten customers, covering orders, planning, material, purchasing, production execution, quality, finished goods, shipment and machine maintenance.

Business rules (defaults; when a user states a different condition, follow the user):
- The business date is 2027-02-28; "now" and "today" mean this date. Records from November 2026 to February 2027 have happened and March work orders are the plan; inventory_balances is the 2027-02-28 snapshot.
- The requested date is sales_orders.requested_delivery_date (one date per order). Units owed = order line ordered_quantity − shipped quantity; once fully shipped, a line is late if its last shipment is after the requested date, days late = last shipment date − requested date; a line still short after its requested date is also late. A work order rejected at inspection can be remade by a replacement work order for the same order line.
- Material requirement = work order quantity × quantity_per × (1 + scrap_factor), rounded up; issued ≥ requirement is complete. Material may be issued only from receipts whose inspection_status is accepted; issuing from a receipt in inspection or rejected is an improper issue.
- A work order is blocked when its BOM revision is not frozen, or when its production plan line's planned_start_date has arrived and material is incomplete. One whose planned start has not arrived and has no material issued yet is not blocked.
- Execution compliance (all four must hold for every execution): the machine's capability for the step's required_capability_id is qualified; the machine has no maintenance work order on the start date (started_date); the operator's qualification for that capability is qualified with valid_to on or after the start date; the operator is assigned, in the weekly shift covering the start date, to the operation's work_center_id.
- Work order completed quantity = good quantity of the last operation; operation yield = good ÷ (good + scrap); work order yield = completed quantity ÷ work order quantity.
- Quality release: the lot is accepted and every measured value lies within the referenced specification's (quality_specification_id) limits, inclusive. A result recorded as pass with a value out of limits counts as a failure and is a quality escape. A product's current specification is the one whose status is active.
- Only accepted finished goods receipts can ship. Purchase orders are measured against expected_receipt_date: a later receipt is late; an order whose expected date is before the business date and whose accepted quantity is below the ordered quantity is overdue, and rejected receipts do not count.
- Available stock = on hand − allocated; stock in the inspection hold cannot be issued. A maintenance work order planned before the business date and not completed is overdue; a machine under repair cannot be scheduled.
- Plans are summed from production plan lines by the month of each work order's due date and compared with that month's forecast; coverage = planned ÷ forecast.

How to answer:
- Answer every question with Cypher; this sample has no functions. Executions reach their work order and work centre through process_steps → manufacturing_operations; inspection lots, finished goods receipts and shipments carry production_order_id, and shipments also carry sales_order_line_id.
- Cypher supports joins, WITH, CASE, aggregation and ORDER BY, not variable-length paths or UNWIND. Dates are YYYY-MM-DD strings and compare directly; numbers may come back as strings. Status values are English codes (completed, blocked, accepted, rejected, pending_inspection, qualified, suspended).

